  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA trough will start to approach from the west as a surface low \nbegins to deepen off the East Coast Sunday. This low will \ngradually ride up the coast through the day on Sunday which will\nlead to increasing rain chances from southwest to northeast as \nthe better forcing for ascent arrives late in the day or in the \nevening.\n\nVarious deterministic models and ensembles have shown better\nagreement today with the low track and associated heavy rain\nshield. The heaviest rainfall still looks to be on the coastal\nplain with lighter amounts farther inland. However, there are\nstill several ensemble members and a few deterministics that\nshow heavy rain spreading farther inland. On the flip side, a\nfew of these models (including the 12z deterministic ECMWF) keep\nthe heavy rain offshore while offering a lighter rain to the\nregion. The low pressure system currently off the coast of FL is\nassociated with a good deal of convection. At times this can \nfool with guidance initializations and cause a less than \naccurate forecast. Will continue with the consensus for QPF and \nlikely have to adjust some going forward. Either way we don't \nthink we're dealing with any flooding issues given the current \ndrought. The rain departs by around 12z Monday as the system \ncontinues to accelerate northward.\n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area early in the\nday on Monday will will lead to winds shifting from out of the \nsoutheast to out of the northwest, becoming breezy by the \nafternoon hours. Forecast soundings suggest the potential for \nsome sites see 25 to 30 knot gusts with good mixing expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter a brief stretch of dry and cooler weather Tuesday-Wednesday, \neyes turn to the next system expected to impact the Northeast around \nThanksgiving day, although exact timing and duration remains \nuncertain. Guidance currently indicates rather favorable dynamics \nfor a strengthening coastal low, with a dual jet streak \nstructure off the east coast, and a strong ridge over SE Canada,\nwhich may increase the odds for cold air locally to provide \nsome wintry precipitation chances, and potentially strengthen \nthe pressure gradient to introduce some strong winds. The EPS \nand GEFS ensembles are beginning to hone in on a pass near the \nbenchmark, indicating a nor'easter type track.\n\nSome key differences exist between each set of members at this time. \nThe EPS members are stronger and provide a deeper initial supply of \ncold air, that could introduce wintry precipitation, possibly \naccumulating snow, into the picture. The GEFS is not only a weaker \nset of members currently, but also brings much milder air into the \nregion with more limited chances for wintry precipitation, partially \ndue to a more easterly onshore wind direction (as opposed to \nnortherly). Inconsistencies in timing continue but it looks like \nThanksgiving into Black Friday will be a stormy/unsettled two days. \nChances for snow/winter precipitation will be highest over the \ninterior/mountains, but cannot be ruled out all the way to the coast \nif a colder solution verifies. The other concern will be the coastal \nflood potential, as tides will be astronomically high, and there \nwill also be the potential for a long fetch/strong onshore winds \ngiven the pressure gradient in place, in addition to rough surf/high \nwaves.",
